{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And HaShem came from Sinai, and shone forth from Seir to them, etc.</b> It should have said 'to Sinai He came.' And Rashi explained that He went forth to meet them like a bridegroom, etc. Yet it is still difficult: why 'from Sinai He came' and not from the place of His dwelling, the heavens? Also in need of resolution: why did He mention a shining forth at Seir and an appearance at Paran? Also difficult according to the opinion of our Rabbis of blessed memory, who said (Sifrei 2b): the Holy One, blessed be He, went around to the children of Esau and Ishmael to offer them the Torah and they did not accept it — why did He mention Seir and Mount Paran and not mention them by their names, Esau and Ishmael? Also the word לָמוֹ ('to them') needs resolution, for some say the word לָמוֹ refers back to Israel, for since the nations did not wish to accept the Torah and Israel did accept it, therefore the shining and the appearance were added for Israel — but the language of the verse does not convey this. And it appears that since all these names are associated with a particular nation — for Seir is associated with Esau, a hairy man (Genesis 27:11), and Mount Paran is associated with Ishmael, of whom it is said (ibid. 16:12): ״וְהוּא יִהְיֶה פֶּרֶא אָדָם״, and פֶּרֶא (wild ass) is derived from the root of פָּארָן — therefore HaShem did not cause the radiance of the revelation of His Presence to shine upon those two nations themselves, for the Holy One, blessed be He, knew that they would not end up accepting the Torah, and He came to them only in order that they should not have an opening of the mouth to say, 'Had He given it to us we would have accepted it.' Therefore HaShem did not manifest His Presence upon the nations, for they were not worthy of that, but He manifested upon them from those places associated with those nations. And with this the language 'and shone forth from Seir to them' is precisely accounted for. Yet we still need to be informed why here He used the term 'shining forth' and here 'appearance,' and here He mentioned לָמוֹ but with the appearance He did not mention לָמוֹ." "And it appears that this is its meaning: for the term 'shining forth' (זְרִיחָה) we find in connection with the sun, as in ״וְזָרַח הַשֶּׁמֶשׁ״ (Ecclesiastes 1:5), and many similar cases. But the term 'appearance' (הוֹפָעָה) we find only in connection with the revelation of His Presence, blessed be He: ״יוֹשֵׁב הַכְּרוּבִים הוֹפִיעָה״ (Psalms 80:2). And since Ishmael repented but Esau did not, therefore He shone forth from Seir — which is associated with Esau — with a 'shining forth' compared to the shining of the light of the sun, and He further alluded to Esau, who counts by the sun. And therefore לָמוֹ is stated, for that scant radiance did reach 'to them' — and perhaps He literally shone the sun upon them. But for Ishmael, who repented in his lifetime, He added the term 'appearance,' that is, the revelation of His Presence from Mount Paran, which is associated with them; yet they did not merit in any case that that great light should reach them literally. Therefore לָמוֹ is not stated, for it did not reach them, but they saw the light on Mount Paran from afar. But Esau, since that light was merely solar light, therefore 'He shone forth from Seir to them' literally. But to Israel He came from Mount Sinai without any shining forth or appearance, for to the nations it was necessary to come by way of shining and appearance, to speak with them in radiant countenance — lest they be tempted to accept the Torah, so that they would not have an opening of the mouth to say, 'Had He come to us in radiant countenance we would have accepted it.' But Israel, whose souls longed for the Torah and loved it greatly — it was not necessary to shine or appear upon them, but rather 'from Sinai He came,' a simple coming. And at first HaShem descended upon Mount Sinai and from there came to Israel, for that mountain is associated with Israel, for it is the lowest of the mountains and Israel are the fewest of all the peoples." "And as they said (Avot 1:1): 'Moses received Torah from Sinai' — and they did not say 'upon Sinai' — but rather because Moses, in the abundance of his humility, considered himself among the lowly, saying, 'What am I that the Torah should be given through me?' But when he heard the word of HaShem: ״בְּהוֹצִיאֲךָ אֶת הָעָם מִמִּצְרַיִם תַּעַבְדוּן אֶת הָאֱלֹהִים עַל הָהָר הַזֶּה״ (Exodus 3:12), then he took to heart: why specifically 'upon this mountain'? Are there no mountains in the world higher than it? Rather, such is the attribute of the Holy One, blessed be He, that He chooses most to dwell among the crushed and lowly of spirit — behold, from then on Moses no longer refused his mission, and he accepted upon himself that the Torah would be given through him. Therefore it is stated: 'Moses received Torah from Sinai,' for from Sinai he learned this — that he was worthy to receive the Torah more than anyone else. And this is what our Rabbis of blessed memory said (Megillah 29a) on the verse ״לָמָּה תְרַצְּדוּן הָרִים גַּבְנֻנִּים״ (Psalms 68:17): 'All of you are blemished in comparison to Mount Sinai,' as you say: ״אוֹ גִבֵּן אוֹ דַק״ (Leviticus 21:20). And what blemish did they find in those mountains, or what relevance has a hunchback to mountains? For a hunchback is a blemish of the eyes — but rather He hinted here that haughtiness of heart is a great blemish in the Torah of HaShem, and these mountains, by virtue of being high — that is a blemish in them. And correspondingly in a person, raised eyes are a blemish of the eye; therefore He brings proof from ״אוֹ גִבֵּן״. And therefore these mountains are not associated with Israel, only Mount Sinai, and upon it HaShem descended first, for by this He showed them that the Holy One, blessed be He, dwells most among the crushed, and they should take proof from here that it was not because of your being multitudes among all the peoples that HaShem desired you, for you are the fewest — and therefore they are fitting for the Torah and the Torah is fitting for them. Therefore it is stated: 'HaShem came from Sinai,' and this is indeed great praise for Israel, that they are worthy to receive the blessings." "<b>And from myriads of holiness.</b> Some say: He came from among myriads of holy angels who said: A hidden treasure are You, coming to give it to flesh and blood. Yet nevertheless the Holy One, blessed be He, came from them, because they are holy and have within them no evil impulse, no adultery, and no robbery. Therefore, from the aspect of their being 'myriads of holiness,' He came to Israel to supply their deficiency and to refine the rust of their matter, like gold refined in fire. Therefore it is stated: ״מִימִינוֹ אֵשׁ דָּת לָמוֹ״ — in order to refine them so that they do not incline to the left but only to the right, for ״לֵב חָכָם לִימִינוֹ״ (Ecclesiastes 10:2)."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 33, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:16:41.898360189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}